{0 Observe}

Observe is a portable structured logging core for OCaml. It owns point and
wide-log authoring, admission, canonical completion, formatting, diagnostics,
additional drains, and deterministic capture without performing I/O.

Point logs auto-emit one text, anonymous structured, or declared record event.
Wide logs use the same field and schema meaning but accept successive lazy
contributions before one explicit or scope-bounded completion. The core owns
current-handle meaning and operation behavior; runtime compositions supply the
dynamic binding and effect settlement required to execute them.
Drains that accept work for later effectful delivery can report a terminal
worker failure through {!val:Observe.Drain.Integration.report_failure} without
recursing through normal logging.

{1 Installation}

Link the portable core with [(libraries observe)]. Add
[(preprocess (pps observe.ppx))] when using the deriver or untyped-value extension.
Configurable Lwt I/O and the ready Unix composition are distributed in the
[observe-lwt] and [observe-lwt-unix] packages.

{1 Authoring}

Ordinary code logs through {!module:Observe.Logs}:

{[
[%observe.info text ~tag:"auth" "User logged in"];

[%observe.debug text ~tag:"query" "%s" (explain_query query)];

[%observe.info
  untyped { action = "user_login"; user_id = 42 }];

[%observe.info typed ~using:event_schema event]
]}

The callback and every text, untyped, or typed value constructed inside it run
only after an active output path and level admission. A configured drain route
predicate runs later because it inspects the completed safe log. Before production
initialization, logs outside an active capture are withheld and counted rather
than raising an initialization exception.

A portable wide log is explicit:

{[
let log = Observe.Logs.create ~name:"checkout" () in
[%observe.set log { cart_id = string cart_id }];
[%observe.set log { phase = "authorized" }];
[%observe.info log "authorization completed"];
Observe.Logs.emit log
]}

For a record derived with [@@deriving observe], [event_schema] locks complete
point values and sparse wide patches to the same declared root. The deriver also
generates [event_patch] and one-layer
[[%observe.set log typed { ... }]] authoring.

{1 Configuration and output}

{!val:Observe.Config.create} returns a validated result;
{!val:Observe.Config.create_exn} raises
{!exception:Observe.Config.Invalid_configuration}. Automatic console output is
automatic formatted output. {!module:Observe.Drain} values are additional
application-owned outputs that receive completed {!type:Observe.Log.t} values.
[Config.Silent] suppresses the console, not drains. [Config.Auto] chooses
pretty development output or production NDJSON from the environment.
[Config.create] accepts each configured behavior as a named value of its own
type. Omitting [~min_level] admits [Info] and above. The default does not guess
which application values are sensitive or activate sensitivity rules.

{!module:Observe.Logs.Enricher} values contribute reusable structured context
to the same canonical fields as caller authoring. Their callbacks run only for
admitted completion. Caller fields win ordinary collisions; composition may
grant one enricher authority over named non-reserved root fields. Duplicate
enricher names and overlapping authority are configuration errors. Enricher
callbacks are synchronous and may run concurrently for different observations,
so they must be safe for concurrent invocation. An admitted Observe log emitted
by a callback can invoke the same enricher again; the callback owns termination
of that recursion. Ordinary failures are contained, configured limit exhaustion
becomes bounded truncation or omission, and runtime control exceptions propagate
unchanged.

{!module:Observe.Logs.Limits} owns finite canonical-materialization bounds.
{!val:Observe.Logs.Limits.default} is active when [~limits] is omitted;
configuration may choose another validated finite value, but cannot disable
the bounds. Total-size accounting charges canonical nodes, object fields, list
entries, payload bytes, truncation markers, and required completed-log metadata;
the exact public units are documented on {!val:Observe.Logs.Limits.create}.

{1 Sampling, completion retention, and routing}

{!module:Observe.Logs.Sampling} configures validated per-level base rates.
Omitting [~sampling] is inert, and omitted rates retain every log. Errors are
therefore retained by default unless [~error] is explicitly configured.
[Correlation_stable] aligns the base draw for related parent, child, and point
observations without turning them into one atomic retention unit.

{!module:Observe.Logs.Retention} can rescue one completed log before fan-out.
Its callback receives only the bounded log after global disclosure safety.
Returning [false] defers to base sampling and cannot force a drop. Ordinary
callback failures are diagnosed and fail open; runtime control exceptions remain
native.

{!val:Observe.Drain.with_route} selects one drain from that same safe immutable
log. Nested routes form a conjunction. A route failure skips only its drain, and
{!val:Observe.Drain.with_redaction} can then strengthen disclosure policy on the
selected branch. Ordinary route exceptions are contained; runtime control
exceptions remain native. Route and drain callbacks run synchronously, may run
concurrently for different observations, and must promptly return. Capture
bypasses probabilistic retention and external routing while preserving level
admission, finite materialization, and global redaction.

{1 Disclosure-safe redaction}

Redaction is an explicit caller policy. Omitting [~redaction] leaves values
unchanged; Observe does not infer sensitivity from names, values, or the
environment. A policy is inert until selected by [Config] or a destination
drain.

Exact paths address the canonical structured root, including nested objects,
list positions, and variant cases. A policy associated with [~using] is checked
against one declared schema and can be reused by typed point logs and
schema-locked wide logs:

{[
module R = Observe.Logs.Redaction

let typed_redaction =
  R.create_exn
    ~using:checkout_event_schema
    ~rules:
      [ R.Rule.at
          (R.Path.root
          |> R.Path.field "payment"
          |> R.Path.case "Authorized"
          |> R.Path.field "authorization_id")
          (R.Action.mask
             (R.Mask.keep_suffix ~characters:4 ~hidden:(R.Mask.Fill "*") ()))
      ; R.Rule.at
          (R.Path.root |> R.Path.field "item_count")
          R.Action.remove
      ]
    ()

let config =
  Observe.Config.create_exn ~service:"orders"
    ~redaction:typed_redaction ()
]}

The same hierarchy supports open structured paths and value matchers. Matching
rules can replace or mask matching structured scalars, point-text messages, and
wide annotation messages; structural removal is available only for an exact
structured path. Known typed paths and string-mask targets are validated during
policy construction. Opaque custom mapped descriptions defer exact lookup to
safe runtime traversal, while unstable positions such as hash-table iteration
indexes are rejected. String masks are finite and operate on UTF-8 scalar boundaries.
The finite prefix, suffix, and ends masks always hide at least one scalar from a
non-empty source. A custom mask receives only the already bounded matched
string. Ordinary callback failures and invalid or over-budget results use the
safe fallback or withhold the affected branch; a callback that never returns
cannot be preempted by portable OCaml, so custom callback termination remains
the caller's responsibility. Runtime control exceptions remain native.

Redaction cannot rewrite package metadata such as service, version, timestamp,
level, tag, schema identity, operation names or identifiers, parent identity, or
annotation timestamps. The effective policy runs after canonical
materialization and before capture, presentation, or drains. JSON and pretty
output render a successful replacement as its ordinary safe value, while
capture can inspect bounded effect and location evidence through
{!val:Observe.Log.redactions} without receiving the removed source.

{!val:Observe.Drain.with_redaction} adds a stricter policy to one destination
branch. The destination receives only the already globally safe observation and
cannot recover an earlier removal. Conflicting exact actions are rejected when
the policy is built; identical rules are deduplicated and declaration order is
not semantic. A dynamic conflict or traversal failure fails closed locally and
records a bounded diagnostic rather than delivering the raw observation.
Use {!val:Observe.Logs.Redaction.combine} or
{!val:Observe.Logs.Redaction.combine_exn} to select typed and global reusable
policies together; composition rejects known conflicts and does not make list
order semantic.

{1 I/O composition and capture}

{!module-type:Observe.IO.S} owns effects, dynamic binding, cleanup, native
control exceptions, the wall clock, occurrence identity, sampling draws,
console presentation capability, and console offers. {!module:Observe.Make}
consumes that completed contract without adding a runtime or operating-system
dependency to the core.

{!val:Observe.Make.with_capture} installs capture as the innermost
dynamic route, suppresses production delivery for the callback, restores the
previous binding, and closes the capture exactly once.

{1 Safety boundary}

Every published point or wide log contains one package-owned immutable
snapshot. Canonical freezing applies finite configured limits to depth, object
width, collection length, nodes, individual strings and byte values, and
deterministic total size before capture or output. Safe prefixes and siblings
survive localized truncation. Caller mutation after emission cannot change
later inspection or delivery. Unsafe, cyclic, unsupported, invalid, or raising
projection paths are withheld and diagnosed without a live-value fallback.
Runtime control exceptions remain native.

Descriptions generated by [@@deriving observe] retain presentation distinctions
that JSON intentionally collapses, including strings versus nullary
constructors and ordinary versus polymorphic constructors. Their bounded direct
freezer creates format-neutral canonical meaning; official formatters consume
only that result. Decoding, comparison, binary encoding, and the remaining
machine operations stay with Repr.
{!val:Observe.Type.repr} exposes that machine for Repr consumers. Raw Repr
descriptions are not lifted into [Observe.Type.t] because they do not prove a
bounded canonical freezer.

The portable core owns no background writer or durability guarantee. The ready
Lwt-Unix package owns a bounded serialized console worker and exposes explicit
[flush] and [shutdown] lifecycle operations. See the repository README for
ready, test-capture, and custom-integration examples.
